From c8631e52484a80c36f4ddb6a18e31e219fb0c33d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ananya Sridhar <ananyasridhar108@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 03:45:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Verbal instructions: Indian-accent voice preference, nav
 screens go manual-only, finish the remaining 5 bespoke Chime games
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three follow-ups to the verbal-instructions feature, based on direct
feedback after it shipped:

1. Voice preference (lib/speech.js): speak() now asks for an en-IN voice
   via speechSynthesis.getVoices() (caching the list once
   onvoiceschanged fires, since it's often empty synchronously on first
   call), falling back to utter.lang = 'en-IN' as a hint, and silently to
   the device default if neither is available. Worth being upfront in the
   code comment about what this actually is: there's no server-side TTS
   provider wired into this app, no API key, no billing — this is the
   browser's free built-in Web Speech API, so actual voice availability is
   whatever the device exposes, not something any client code can force.
   Chrome on Android/desktop commonly lists a network "Google" en-IN voice
   online; iOS/macOS Safari ships a built-in "Rishi" en-IN voice offline
   too — devices with neither just get their normal default voice.

   Found that VillageBuilder.jsx had already independently built almost
   this exact same pickIndianEnglishVoice() function, wired to a
   manual-only "hear the word" button with no auto-speak. Removed the
   duplicate and consolidated it onto the shared utility, then added the
   auto-speak-once this game was missing (start-screen instruction, and
   each new target word as the round progresses) — one implementation
   instead of two, and it gained the same auto+replay behavior every other
   game already had.

2. Nav/menu screens (Landing.jsx, Play.jsx, GamePicker.jsx) are now
   manual, tap-to-hear only — the auto-play on every visit to a login/menu
   screen was flagged as an annoying voice-over. Converted their
   useSpokenInstruction() calls to plain speak() wired only to the
   existing speaker-icon buttons; no more auto-firing there. This judgment
   call is scoped to nav/menu screens only — the actual games (BreathQuest
   levels, Chime, VaakMirror) still auto-speak once per level/attempt,
   since there it's instructional rather than narrating a menu, and that
   distinction is now written into the code comments at each site so a
   future change doesn't accidentally blur the two again.

3. Finished the remaining 5 bespoke Chime games flagged as a follow-up
   last time (RocketLaunch and SubmarineDive were already done): FireflyJar,
   WindChimeGarden, BubbleWrapPop, and VillageBuilder now all auto-speak
   their start-screen instruction once per entry, held off when the
   game's own existing mute toggle is on (same as every other game),
   with a replay button next to the instruction text.

Verified: npm run build clean (664 modules, 0 errors) and oxlint clean
(0 errors, same 3 pre-existing harmless fast-refresh warnings) after every
change; grepped for and confirmed no leftover references to
VillageBuilder's removed pickIndianEnglishVoice() or any stale
speakTargetWord call sites; diffed all 10 changed files line by line.
